// demos/mcview/main.fi -- joins a real Minecraft server (offline mode), waits until the chunks have settled and
// renders the world around the player with mc_render_world + mc_raster into a PNG (CPU only, no window).
//
//   live:    mcview [flags] <host> <port> <name> <out-dir> [view=6] [settle-ms=3000] [yaw=0] [pitch=20] [protocol=763] [up=2]
//   replay:  mcview [flags] --replay <out-dir> <world-file> [yaw] [pitch]
//   flags (anywhere on the command line, not counted as positional arguments):
//     --sky   sky pass (mc_sky: gradient, sun / moon, sky fog colour, light tint of the world pixels)
//     --box   selection box (mc_input.input_draw_box) around the block under the crosshair
//     --hud   crosshair, hotbar and debug lines (mc_hud)
//     MCVIEW_SKY_APPROX=1 (env): Sky.approx (sunset glow / gradient approximated, <= 1 level, ~2.5x faster at sunset); default exact = the reference
//     MCVIEW_GFX=vk (env): the frame is drawn through the Vulkan back end (mc.vk_mc) and read back (default cpu; no Vulkan device = cpu with a message).
//              Not bit equal to the CPU raster (PSNR gate tools/mc/play/run_vkview.sh); run with MCVIEW_WORKERS=0 (stream threads crash libvulkan_lvp).
//     MCVIEW_STREAM=1 (env): mc_stream with MCVIEW_WORKERS (3) mesher threads draws the world instead of mc_render_world (hash may differ: culling).
//     --all   all three.   Without flags the image is exactly the plain world render (hash equal to the python renderer).
//   Frame order: raster_clear -> sky_draw -> sky_set_raster_fog -> world -> sky_tint_frame -> selection box -> HUD.
//
// live mode writes <out-dir>/world.mcw (the received world, text, run length coded, format below) and then renders
// it exactly like replay mode does. Replay renders a world file WITHOUT a server (offline test, fixtures).
// Outputs in <out-dir>: view.png, view.rgba (raw framebuffer), view.txt (camera as f64 bit patterns, mesher flags,
// the block tables of every state id of the world, the Firn image hash, metrics), atlas.bin.
// tools/mc/view/ref_render.py renders the same world with its OWN mesher + the python rasterizer and compares.
//
// Resource packs (off by default): --pack=a.zip,b.zip and/or env MC_PACKS / MC_PACK_CONFIG / MC_PACK_AUTO / MC_PACK_DIR / MC_PACK_TILE (see
//              mc_packs.res_setup_env). Extra: MCVIEW_PACK_TICK=n (animation tick), MCVIEW_PACK_RELOAD=1 (hot reload between frames 1 and 2).
// Environment: MCVIEW_GREEDY=1 (greedy meshing; default 0 = one quad per face, the python reference needs this)
//              MCVIEW_FOG=0 (fog off; default on)   MCVIEW_W / MCVIEW_H (image size, default 640x360)
//              MCVIEW_TICKS (time of day 0..23999, default 2000)  MCVIEW_RAIN (0..256, default 0)  MCVIEW_REACH (pick reach in
//              blocks, default 24)  MCVIEW_SLOT (hotbar slot 0..8, default 0)  MCVIEW_FPS (value shown in the debug lines;
//              default = measured from the cached frame; set it for a reproducible image hash)
//
// World file (text, LF): `mcworld 1`, `protocol <n>`, `min_y <n>`, `cam <x> <y> <z> <yaw> <pitch>` (f64 bit patterns,
// hex), `view <chunks>`, `chunks <N>`; per chunk `C <x> <z> <nsec>` followed by one line per section:
// `U <i> <id>` (uniform) or `R <i> <runs> <id> <len> <id> <len> ...` (runs over the 4096 ids, index (y*16+z)*16+x).
// Chunks ascending by x, then z.
//
// Live mode applies unload packets (column removed from the world, its meshes freed via wr_drop_chunk).
// Nothing here names a packet id: positions come from mc_client (last Synchronize Player Position), chunks from
// mc_chunk. Versions without chunk columns of the post-1.9 kind (1.8 bulk) are not supported by this demo.

